  "prediction": "The report discusses the Fire Management Assistance Grant (FMAG) program under the Robert T. Stafford Disaster Relief and Emergency Assistance Act. FMAGs provide federal assistance for fire suppression activities on state and tribal lands when these fires may become major disasters. The grants cover equipment, personnel, and other expenses, with a cost-share requirement of 25% for states and tribes. FMAGs can be requested by states or tribes when they determine that a fire may become a major disaster. The report answers frequently asked questions about the FMAG process, including how to request assistance, the evaluation criteria for FMAG declarations, and the types of assistance provided. The report also notes that tribes have the ability to request FMAGs, and that some tribal lands receive fire suppression support directly from the National Interagency Fire Center without cost-sharing requirements. The report concludes by explaining that FMAGs are not available for fires on federal lands, but may be available for fires on mixed federal and non-federal lands under certain circumstances.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report discusses the Fire Management Assistance Grant (FMAG) program under the Robert T. Stafford Disaster Relief and Emergency Assistance Act. FMAGs provide federal assistance for fire suppression activities on state and tribal lands when these fires may become major disasters. The grants cover equipment, personnel, and other expenses, with a cost-share requirement of 25% for states and tribes. FMAGs can be requested by states or tribes when they determine that a fire may become a major disaster. The report answers frequently asked questions about the FMAG process, including how to request assistance, the evaluation criteria for FMAG declarations, and the types of assistance provided. The report also notes that tribes have the ability to request FMAGs, and that some tribal lands receive fire suppression support directly from the National Interagency Fire Center without cost-sharing requirements. The report concludes by explaining that FMAGs are not available for fires on federal lands, but may be available for fires on mixed federal and non-federal lands under certain circumstances.",
  "score": 40.196077931805085,
  "answers": [
    "Section 420 of the Robert T. Stafford Disaster Relief and Emergency Assistance Act (P.L. 93-288, hereinafter the Stafford Act) authorizes the President to \"declare\" a Fire Management Assistance Grant (FMAG). In the interest of saving time, the authority to make the declaration has been delegated to the Federal Emergency Management Agency's (FEMA's) Regional Administrators. Once issued, the FMAG declaration authorizes various forms of federal fire suppression assistance such as the provision of equipment, personnel, and grants to state, local, and tribal governments for the control, management, and mitigation of any fire on certain public or private forest land or grassland that might become a major disaster. This federal assistance requires a cost-sharing component such that state, local, and tribal governments are responsible for 25% of the expenses. This report answers frequently asked questions about FMAGs. This report will be updated as events warrant."
  ],
